# 2. Problem Statement

Modern AI models are good enough to operate a large share of routine company work. They do not, because they lack the company-specific know-how required to act correctly:

* The *real* refund policy (including the exceptions never written down)
* Who must approve a pricing deviation, and above what threshold
* The exact runbook engineers follow when a specific service degrades
* The unwritten conventions a new hire only learns by asking someone

This knowledge exists, but it is:

| Where knowledge lives | Why agents can't use it |
| --- | --- |
| People's heads | Never written down; lost when people leave |
| Email / Slack threads | Buried, contradictory, undated, no single source of truth |
| Support tickets | Implicit procedures encoded in thousands of one-off resolutions |
| Internal docs / wikis | Stale, partial, written for humans not execution |
| Databases / tools | Structured but lack the rules for *when* and *how* to act |

Companies function because humans vaguely remember where the knowledge is and how to apply it. Agents cannot operate that way. They need the procedure, made explicit, current, and executable.

Document search and RAG chatbots do not solve this. They retrieve text for a human to read and interpret. They do not produce an executable procedure an agent can follow, and they have no notion of whether that procedure is still correct.

